And so I feel like I've got a better handle on it now as to what tends to work with this technology, what tends not to work with this technology,

31
00:02:47,710 --> 00:02:52,550
but also a better filter on what is pure hype and what actually has some basis in reality.

32
00:02:52,550 --> 00:02:56,949
And I'm really I'm interested in what's real and what works.

33
00:02:56,949 --> 00:03:07,229
So when I talk about myself being an AI realist, what I mean is I'm just focused on what's real, what works, what's economically viable, what's environmentally viable, and what's ethical,

34
00:03:07,229 --> 00:03:11,630
etc. And what kind of conclusions did you reach if you reach any so far at least?

35
00:03:11,640 --> 00:03:13,670
Well, it's an ongoing story, isn't it?

36
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:22,190
But I think the technology itself, as with all things physical, is going to have its limits and I think we are approaching its limits.

37
00:03:22,190 --> 00:03:31,509
I think no matter how much you scale language models, reliability in particular is always going to be a problem and I don't think it's going to get much better.

38
00:03:31,509 --> 00:03:40,750
I feel at the moment that they're probably rough They may get more powerful and more capable, but I don't think they're going to get much more reliable.

39
00:03:40,750 --> 00:03:45,030
And I think the problem that we have as software developers is how unreliable they are.

40
00:03:45,040 --> 00:03:50,190
How long we can leave them to get on with it before we have to check and we have to intervene.

41
00:03:50,190 --> 00:03:51,990
And I think that's going to be the real speed limit.

42
00:03:52,000 --> 00:04:01,270
You found some things, some practices or techniques that help in making these AI agents,

43
00:04:01,270 --> 00:04:04,590
AI tools a bit more reliable or let's say more effective when used in software development.

44
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:08,630
I think maybe a bit less unreliable is the word that we're looking for here.

45
00:04:08,640 --> 00:04:11,910
So, from a purely practical point of view,

46
00:04:11,920 --> 00:04:20,710
there are a number of effects that people have observed with large language models that tends to make the predictions they make more accurate and more reliable.

47
00:04:20,720 --> 00:04:25,190
And one of the big ones is the size of the context that they're working with.

48
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:30,031
So, the amount of information that's being fed in large language models are stateless. They're like web servers.

49
00:04:30,040 --> 00:04:36,790
You're having to either pass state back and forth, which is how we work with GPT-5 or Claude Opus or whatever.

50
00:04:36,790 --> 00:04:40,270
They have no memory of our conversation in the same way that a web server doesn't.

51
00:04:40,280 --> 00:04:43,909
That memory has to be managed and retrieved somehow.

52
00:04:43,909 --> 00:04:50,670
And the longer the conversation goes on or the more information we feed in, the worse they tend to perform in terms of reliability.

53
00:04:50,680 --> 00:04:54,310
So, there are all kinds of effects that kick in.

54
00:04:54,310 --> 00:04:56,790
One big one is what they call attention dilution.

55
00:04:56,790 --> 00:05:05,950
So, the way that language model works is the context, they have to calculate what's called attention between pretty much every token in the context,

56
00:05:05,950 --> 00:05:08,030
a statistical relationship between every token.

57
00:05:08,030 --> 00:05:16,990
Now, if that's a thousand tokens, then that's a million calculations and a million probabilities that come out the other end.

58
00:05:16,990 --> 00:05:19,350
If it's a hundred thousand tokens, then that's a massive calculation.

59
00:05:19,350 --> 00:05:23,430
And all these probabilities ultimately have to add up to one.

60
00:05:23,430 --> 00:05:26,871
That's the way the probability works. And so,

61
00:05:26,871 --> 00:05:37,110
the more token attentions you're having to the smaller and smaller and smaller the probabilities get until they become insignificant.

62
00:05:37,110 --> 00:05:41,390
At which point, nuanced detail, that kind of stuff just gets completely lost.

63
00:05:41,390 --> 00:05:47,150
So, they don't perform very well with large No matter what the vendors say is the maximum token window,

64
00:05:47,160 --> 00:05:53,190
the reality is there some very good studies that back this up that found this.

65
00:05:53,190 --> 00:05:57,790
The reality is the effective maximum token limit is orders of magnitude small.

66
00:05:57,790 --> 00:06:05,630
The point at which accuracy and reliability starts dropping off a cliff is in the order of hundreds to maybe the low thousands of tokens,

67
00:06:05,640 --> 00:06:08,430
maybe 100 to 1,000.

68
00:06:08,430 --> 00:06:13,030
When they say, "Oh, we can go up to a 400,000 tokens or even a million tokens."

69
00:06:13,030 --> 00:06:17,430
Yeah, but you're not going to get a lot of out of sense out of the model at that kind of scale.

70
00:06:17,440 --> 00:06:27,910
And it's all, I guess, one of the things we need to do is to use these models in such a way that we somehow minimize the context. How do we do that?

71
00:06:27,919 --> 00:06:30,630
What kind of techniques do we use to do that?

72
00:06:30,640 --> 00:06:37,150
The ways that we broke problems down in Agile software development,

73
00:06:37,150 --> 00:06:43,511
for example, they're not just compatible with AI-assisted and agentic software development, they're kind of essential.

74
00:06:43,520 --> 00:06:45,830
You solve one problem at a time.

75
00:06:45,830 --> 00:06:54,310
And when it comes to managing context or what they call context engineering, the context should only contain the information needed to solve that problem.

76
00:06:54,320 --> 00:07:01,150
You're doing test-driven development, for example, and we've agreed a bunch of tests about an online shopping cart.

77
00:07:01,150 --> 00:07:05,710
So, we've agreed some scenarios, some slices into the behavior of the system.

78
00:07:05,710 --> 00:07:09,830
And the first slice we want to tackle is adding items to the cart.

79
00:07:09,830 --> 00:07:16,470
We might set up a context for writing a test that fails for adding one item to the cart.

80
00:07:16,480 --> 00:07:18,390
That would be the context.

81
00:07:18,390 --> 00:07:26,550
And then when we've seen that test fail, we might flush that context and say, "Okay, now we want the model to write the simplest code that will pass that test."

82
00:07:26,550 --> 00:07:29,590
That's a brand new task and a brand new context.

83
00:07:29,600 --> 00:07:33,750
Writing failing tests writing code to pass tests are not the same thing.

84
00:07:33,750 --> 00:07:42,430
So, you're going through these very short steps where you're constructing the smallest, most specific context possible to execute the current task,

85
00:07:42,430 --> 00:07:44,871
the current step in the process, in the workflow.

86
00:07:44,880 --> 00:07:51,870
And then when it's successfully completed that task, we go with a new context.

87
00:07:51,870 --> 00:07:53,870
We say, "What do we need to know?

88
00:07:53,870 --> 00:07:57,910
What does the model need to know in order to write the code to pass the test?"

89
00:07:57,910 --> 00:08:08,630
Well, it needs to see the code for the test, of course, and it needs to see some existing code that is relevant if there's classes or functions that are related to this that are going to be involved. But it doesn't need to know any more than that.

90
00:08:08,630 --> 00:08:11,190
It needs to know the task.

91
00:08:11,190 --> 00:08:13,430
And very importantly, it needs to know the test.

92
00:08:13,430 --> 00:08:15,470
Which is what are we aiming to achieve here?

93
00:08:15,470 --> 00:08:25,190
And I think people are discovering, and this is something I discovered quite a long time ago, large language models, for two reasons I think, perform much better when you give them tests.

94
00:08:25,190 --> 00:08:32,589
You say, "This is what you need to do, and here's the test or the example that it needs to satisfy." And there are two good reasons for that.

95
00:08:32,589 --> 00:08:34,430
One is that's how they're trained now.

96
00:08:34,430 --> 00:08:40,990
They don't just give you a code snippet from wherever it's come from, GitHub or Stack Overflow.

97
00:08:40,990 --> 00:08:46,110
They will tend to pair them with examples or tests of how that code will be used.

98
00:08:46,110 --> 00:08:49,030
So, you can get a much better match when they're pattern matching.

99
00:08:49,030 --> 00:08:55,270
But also, I think more recently in the last year or so, they're actually using test execution to train the models.

100
00:08:55,270 --> 00:08:57,590
So, here's the example.

101
00:08:57,590 --> 00:08:59,791
You complete the example, please. Let's run the test.

102
00:08:59,800 --> 00:09:03,351
Let's see if the test passed. No, it failed. Let's feed that back.

103
00:09:03,351 --> 00:09:06,910
So, reinforcement learning has evolved, I think.

104
00:09:06,910 --> 00:09:11,670
For that reason, it's not at all surprising that they work well with tests.

105
00:09:11,670 --> 00:09:18,710
But also, if you're working with agentic coding where it might take multiple passes at it, having a test gives it something to converge on.

106
00:09:18,720 --> 00:09:22,350
Without the test, it just goes round and round in circles.

107
00:09:22,350 --> 00:09:31,710
But if you give it a very clear success criteria, very clear exit criteria, the completion rates that I found with closed-loop experiments are significantly higher, a lot higher.

108
00:09:31,720 --> 00:09:34,750
Never 100%, but they're pretty good.

109
00:09:34,750 --> 00:09:42,831
But the main thing about context is once it's achieved its goal, it's written the test, you've seen the test fail,

110
00:09:42,831 --> 00:09:45,671
okay, flush the context.

111
00:09:45,671 --> 00:09:51,070
Now here's a new task that needs a new context that's only related to that task.

112
00:09:51,070 --> 00:09:53,470
And so I sort of break it down into these short steps,

113
00:09:53,480 --> 00:10:01,750
these micro steps that have every single one of which have some kind of explicit exit criteria.

114
00:10:01,750 --> 00:10:07,990
And once it exits, we flush the context and we construct a new context specifically to the next task.

115
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:10,310
Okay.

116
00:10:10,310 --> 00:10:16,830
And how do we compose these creating all these contexts and so coming up with a design of the system that makes some sort of engineering sense?

117
00:10:16,840 --> 00:10:18,670
It's a very good question.

118
00:10:18,670 --> 00:10:26,071
So I went through a journey over the last year or so where I started with me controlling everything. So I was the agent.

119
00:10:26,071 --> 00:10:28,031
Okay, do this. Let's look at the results.

120
00:10:28,031 --> 00:10:31,031
Okay, not happy. Right, let's fix that.

121
00:10:31,031 --> 00:10:33,430
And gradually got more ambitious.

122
00:10:33,430 --> 00:10:37,430
So have you heard of Ralph Loops or Ralph Wiggum Loops?

123
00:10:37,430 --> 00:10:41,390
This is an idea based on a character in The Simpsons who's always dying, always in terrible danger.

124
00:10:41,390 --> 00:10:47,870
And the idea is that you give the agent a goal and it throws the dice once.

125
00:10:47,870 --> 00:10:51,790
And if the number you want doesn't come up, you clear the deck.

126
00:10:51,790 --> 00:10:54,390
You say, "Let's clear the context.

127
00:10:54,390 --> 00:10:57,271
Let's reset the code and try again." So you throw the dice again.

128
00:10:57,280 --> 00:11:00,630
And you throw the dice again. And you throw the dice again.

129
00:11:00,630 --> 00:11:06,350
Most Ralph Wiggum Loops have a sort of a maximum number of tries. I've seen people doing five, 10, three, whatever.

130
00:11:06,360 --> 00:11:10,030
And so I got into Ralph Wiggum Loops which became popular.

131
00:11:10,040 --> 00:11:12,829
And that's a single process basically.

132
00:11:12,839 --> 00:11:18,270
So the agent is just a loop that goes round and round and round. And I had some success with that,

133
00:11:18,270 --> 00:11:26,550
but I was finding that there are times when you're asking the Ralph Loop to throw 13 and it just can't do it.

134
00:11:26,550 --> 00:11:28,990
I call them doom loops.

135
00:11:28,990 --> 00:11:30,630
It just goes round and round and round and and it can't do it.

136
00:11:30,630 --> 00:11:35,150
The problem it's trying to solve is out of its training data distribution.

137
00:11:35,150 --> 00:11:39,070
And sometimes you could solve that by dropping a gear and say,

138
00:11:39,070 --> 00:11:44,950
"Okay, let's go back into planning mode and break the problem down into If you can't throw 13,

139
00:11:44,960 --> 00:11:48,070
try throwing a six and a seven.

140
00:11:48,070 --> 00:11:49,990
They're more probable."

141
00:11:49,990 --> 00:11:51,750
But that doesn't always work.

142
00:11:51,750 --> 00:11:55,510
Sometimes you have to step in and go, "Okay, this problem is just out of the model's distribution.

143
00:11:55,510 --> 00:11:57,350
It's not been trained on this."

144
00:11:57,350 --> 00:12:05,870
You could see this with diffusion models, the text-to-image models, where you ask it things like, "Can you generate an image, please, of a wine glass full to the brim?"

145
00:12:05,880 --> 00:12:08,670
And they just can't do it.

146
00:12:08,670 --> 00:12:18,430
And there are all kinds of examples cuz it's not in the training data distribution that language models just can't do, no matter how many passes you give them. So, you have to be ready to intervene.

147
00:12:18,430 --> 00:12:22,110
You have to watch it like a hawk and go, "Okay, it's thrashing here.

148
00:12:22,110 --> 00:12:24,671
It's in a doom loop. So, I need to intervene."

149
00:12:24,680 --> 00:12:36,190
The goal, I think, of what we might call long-horizon, fully autonomous agentic coding, that we give it a set of requirements and then we go to the beach, I think, is complete fantasy.

150
00:12:36,200 --> 00:12:43,070
I've seen nobody succeeding I've seen people succeeding at having agents run for hours.

151
00:12:43,070 --> 00:12:47,750
What I haven't seen yet is anyone succeed at having them do that and produce something that actually works at the end.

152
00:12:47,760 --> 00:12:50,030
So, it's more context.

153
00:12:50,030 --> 00:12:55,630
Say TDD will give a have the model create a test, then flush the context, create the code that solves the test.

154
00:12:55,630 --> 00:12:57,710
So, basically, trying to keep the context always small.

155
00:12:57,710 --> 00:12:59,310
Then we need to grow this thing.

156
00:12:59,310 --> 00:13:01,430
How do we work?

157
00:13:01,430 --> 00:13:03,150
Do we create one module at a time if we want our modular system?

158
00:13:03,160 --> 00:13:08,190
We do it the way that we've always done it, which is there are two sides of this.

159
00:13:08,190 --> 00:13:10,310
One is the map and one is the terrain.

160
00:13:10,310 --> 00:13:12,070
This is a message that I found really difficult to get across.

161
00:13:12,070 --> 00:13:18,150
Cuz test-driven development and refactoring and continuous integration, they're very much putting one foot in front of the other.

162
00:13:18,160 --> 00:13:22,510
And I think maybe the message we're not getting across is you do have to look where you're going.

163
00:13:22,520 --> 00:13:26,550
And you have to have, at a high level, some kind of route plan.

164
00:13:26,550 --> 00:13:34,670
But there are times when you're not sure, take a step back and just maybe do a little sketch or write it down on paper or whatever.

165
00:13:34,670 --> 00:13:36,510
This is the general road map.

166
00:13:36,510 --> 00:13:43,510
I think now I mean Kent Beck recently published a blog post about what he's calling Canon test-driven development.

167
00:13:43,510 --> 00:13:52,630
And in Canon test-driven development, there are such things as test lists, which he talked about in his I've got it right here just in case in his book on TDD.

168
00:13:52,630 --> 00:13:55,870
He talks about test lists. So you're not going into this cold.

169
00:13:55,870 --> 00:13:58,310
You've actually thought about the problem that you're facing.

170
00:13:58,310 --> 00:14:00,190
And you make a little list, just a high-level sketch.

171
00:14:00,200 --> 00:14:05,230
Remembering of course the map is not the terrain.

172
00:14:05,230 --> 00:14:06,990
And that gives you a way to keep your place.

173
00:14:06,990 --> 00:14:08,630
Okay, that's the step we're doing now.

174
00:14:08,630 --> 00:14:10,710
This is the step we're doing next.

175
00:14:10,710 --> 00:14:17,870
Remembering of course that as we go, we're going to learn and we're going to adapt and that map is going to change.

176
00:14:17,870 --> 00:14:26,071
And so after the fact, we've got practices like refactoring, continuous architecture, and emergent architecture, that kind of deal of let's visualize what we've got. Let's think about it.

177
00:14:26,080 --> 00:14:28,630
Let's talk about it.

178
00:14:28,630 --> 00:14:33,910
And surprise, surprise, when we're doing it with AI, it's exactly the same.

179
00:14:33,920 --> 00:14:45,070
But we need to remember that AI, that large language models, let's be specific, super auto complete, is not capable of that kind of reasoning.

180
00:14:45,080 --> 00:14:47,190
I mean, literally not capable of it.

181
00:14:47,200 --> 00:14:54,750
Language models, and this is a problem with deep neural networks generally, cannot be trained on long-range patterns.

182
00:14:54,760 --> 00:15:03,430
They cannot be trained on things that are far apart on the bigger picture because those probabilities are so low, so insignificant.

183
00:15:03,430 --> 00:15:07,030
And large language models are kind of like averaging machines.

184
00:15:07,030 --> 00:15:08,750
We see this with model collapse.

185
00:15:08,750 --> 00:15:12,190
You take a model's output and train another model on it, and it just gets dumber.

186
00:15:12,190 --> 00:15:19,829
And then you take that model's output and train another model on it, and it gets even dumber because they tend to produce the most probable response.

187
00:15:19,829 --> 00:15:21,870
So all the nuance and the detail gets lost.

188
00:15:21,880 --> 00:15:32,550
So basically what you are saying is that in terms of well, vision, general direction on what the system shape should be is something that humans are good at.

189
00:15:32,550 --> 00:15:36,750
And we here we can use the AI system to actually helping with implementation of these things.

190
00:15:36,760 --> 00:15:49,950
Absolutely, yeah. Maybe we can have the AI if we are exploring architectural ideas with the appropriate models trained on some architectural patterns or something we can

191
00:15:49,950 --> 00:15:52,150
in general high-level we can can use the system to help us,

192
00:15:52,150 --> 00:15:55,830
but we cannot really tell the system this is the architecture going and implement it.

193
00:15:55,840 --> 00:16:03,310
There is an informational disconnect between we could represent architecture at a higher level and they would be able to match patterns in it,

194
00:16:03,310 --> 00:16:06,190
but then the moment you start asking them to generate code,

195
00:16:06,200 --> 00:16:13,190
you're at a much deeper level of representation and I don't see how they can fix that disconnect.

196
00:16:13,190 --> 00:16:17,470
I guess I mean, when we talk about AI, I always talk about actual intelligence.

197
00:16:17,470 --> 00:16:27,350
You can't take the actual intelligence, the ability to see the big picture, and the ability to actually reason, the ability to learn from very small number of of examples, which they just can't do.

198
00:16:27,360 --> 00:16:34,470
And so, basically, whatever we do with the techniques that we use are aimed at keeping this context constrained.

199
00:16:34,470 --> 00:16:39,670
Well, there are other aspects as well, but keeping the context constrained is an important one.

200
00:16:39,670 --> 00:16:41,430
So, without Absolutely, constrained in scope.

201
00:16:41,440 --> 00:16:42,951
In scope, yeah. This is what I mean.

202
00:16:42,960 --> 00:16:44,829
And constrained in time as well.

203
00:16:44,839 --> 00:16:47,150
Yeah. To be as recent as possible.

204
00:16:47,160 --> 00:16:52,870
And you mentioned Agile, so here I talk about practices that we already use, basically, in Agile development.

205
00:16:52,870 --> 00:16:57,950
This TDD, very short feedback loops, small steps,

206
00:16:57,950 --> 00:17:03,790
and then some things that you mentioned also in your writings is also continuous refactoring, continuous code review.

207
00:17:03,790 --> 00:17:13,350
I guess the continuous code review is important because AI is prone sometimes to generate some questionable quality in code, and so we need to make sure that things are proper.

208
00:17:13,350 --> 00:17:20,030
And this also means that we really need to go small because we cannot really review a thousand lines of code at the time easily.

209
00:17:20,040 --> 00:17:23,429
Or we can, just not very well.

210
00:17:23,429 --> 00:17:25,230
Um not in a very kind of focused kind of way.

211
00:17:25,230 --> 00:17:29,110
This is the other side of it, I think, is in terms of workflow.

212
00:17:29,120 --> 00:17:35,270
So, nobody is questioning whether tools like cool code and codex others can produce a lot of code very fast.

213
00:17:35,280 --> 00:17:39,551
Nobody's questioning that's definitely true. Much faster than humans.

214
00:17:39,551 --> 00:17:44,150
The downside of that is that was never the bottleneck in software development.

215
00:17:44,150 --> 00:17:49,710
So, if you let them run, they will produce large change sets, large batches of changes.

216
00:17:49,710 --> 00:17:55,070
They then start hitting these downstream bottlenecks like code review and like testing, and like merging as well.

217
00:17:55,070 --> 00:18:03,750
And so, what we're seeing, and this is there's a big body of evidence being built up on this by CircleCI, um by Faros,

218
00:18:03,750 --> 00:18:12,311
who do the sort of software development telemetry people, by the DORA folks, the DevOps Research and Association research and assessment people, that shows a very clear picture,

219
00:18:12,320 --> 00:18:22,030
which is those initial gains from code being generated faster are being lost downstream to this chaos of things sitting in queues waiting to be reviewed,

220
00:18:22,040 --> 00:18:26,351
and bugs being discovered, fixes being scheduled, and all this stuff.

221
00:18:26,351 --> 00:18:31,230
And on top of which, far more problems are leaking into production than before.

222
00:18:31,230 --> 00:18:36,030
So, outage years and production incidents are up, delays in delivery are up.

223
00:18:36,030 --> 00:18:40,630
This is the other CircleCI data, and as I say, Faros was saying the same thing,

224
00:18:40,640 --> 00:18:45,071
which is I think the way Faros put it in their report, starting is easy,

225
00:18:45,080 --> 00:18:47,710
finishing is hard.

226
00:18:47,710 --> 00:18:55,271
And finishing was always hard, and what generative AI has done is made starting even easier and even faster. It's a firehose.

227
00:18:55,271 --> 00:18:57,070
You're drinking from a firehose.

228
00:18:57,070 --> 00:19:03,031
And when you're drinking from a firehose, the limit isn't the firehose, the limit's you. How much can we drink at a time?

229
00:19:03,040 --> 00:19:08,470
So, you let the firehose go in these very short bursts, one mouthful at a time.

230
00:19:08,470 --> 00:19:12,630
The same it always was, and that was always the real speed limit in software development,

231
00:19:12,630 --> 00:19:17,390
and continues to be how fast can we test code, how fast can we understand code,

232
00:19:17,390 --> 00:19:19,390
comprehension debt.

233
00:19:19,390 --> 00:19:21,510
We're seeing that building up now with CircleCI's data there.

234
00:19:21,510 --> 00:19:28,110
They've been seeing over the last two or three years that the time taken to fix problems in production has been rising.

235
00:19:28,110 --> 00:19:32,270
And I think I suspect a big factor in that is that nobody understands the code anymore.

236
00:19:32,270 --> 00:19:36,830
And there's this gap you have to leap to say before we fix this, we need to understand it.

237
00:19:36,840 --> 00:19:43,110
This is an interesting one because sometimes when you There are some people that say, you know what, it's generated by AI.

238
00:19:43,110 --> 00:19:46,630
We don't really need to understand it as long as it does the right thing.

239
00:19:46,630 --> 00:19:51,510
On the other hand, the problem is that if we don't understand it, we don't even know if the code actually does what we want.

240
00:19:51,520 --> 00:20:00,150
Probability is a big factor in all of this, which is it's all an interaction with a large language model is essentially a throw of the dice.

241
00:20:00,150 --> 00:20:06,750
And if you calculate the probabilities, maybe eight times out of 10, you get the number you want.

242
00:20:06,750 --> 00:20:08,670
That's good for one interaction.

243
00:20:08,670 --> 00:20:15,070
You might go, "Okay, so if we don't get it the first time, we throw it again and and our probabilities go up."

244
00:20:15,070 --> 00:20:20,390
The problem is, and this is where I diverge from a lot of people who are working with this technology who are enthusiasts of it.

245
00:20:20,390 --> 00:20:27,430
The thing that makes me a realist is the chances of what they call sort of long horizon software development,

246
00:20:27,430 --> 00:20:35,950
where I specify 20 features and go to the pub, the chances of the model getting through all of those features with all of the steps involved without falling over,

247
00:20:35,950 --> 00:20:39,230
without taking a wrong turn, and the moment it takes a wrong turn,

248
00:20:39,240 --> 00:20:40,790
you're now building on top of that.

249
00:20:40,800 --> 00:20:42,710
You're now going in the wrong direction.

250
00:20:42,720 --> 00:20:51,070
And I think the chances of that completing successfully are so astronomically remote that I dismiss them out of hand.

251
00:20:51,070 --> 00:20:56,550
I think long horizon fully autonomous agentic coding is a fantasy and a myth.

252
00:20:56,550 --> 00:20:59,630
It's the pot of gold at the end of the rainbow, but the reality is there is no rainbow.

253
00:20:59,630 --> 00:21:01,830
It's a fool's errand.

254
00:21:01,830 --> 00:21:07,430
I'm concerned with the reality, which is, "Okay, let's look at the technology as it is now and what it can do now.

255
00:21:07,430 --> 00:21:09,870
If you're going to use this stuff, what is the smartest way to use it?

256
00:21:09,880 --> 00:21:20,870
As you know, now there is a lot of talk about people managing agents, maybe tens of them working at the same time and agents that work with each other to solve a problem.

257
00:21:20,880 --> 00:21:26,550
And apparently people reviewing the code of the agents or agents reviewing each other code.

258
00:21:26,550 --> 00:21:30,270
So, you think that this system actually is not that viable after all.

259
00:21:30,280 --> 00:21:32,710
I've seen nothing that persuades me.

260
00:21:32,720 --> 00:21:43,510
Some of the larger scale ones done with cursor and done with Claude code, have indeed run for days and weeks with, as they put it, little human intervention,

261
00:21:43,520 --> 00:21:45,790
but they have produced broken software.

262
00:21:45,800 --> 00:21:53,310
The web browser that was done using, I think they said like 100 or more cursor agents.

263
00:21:53,310 --> 00:21:59,310
Someone, I couldn't be bothered, someone used the API on GitHub to go back through the commit history, the build histories.

264
00:21:59,310 --> 00:22:02,030
There were more than 10,000 commits.

265
00:22:02,030 --> 00:22:04,190
And the thing broke at around commit 100.

266
00:22:04,190 --> 00:22:11,430
It was probably broken before that and never worked again until human stepped in and fixed it so that it would at least compile.

267
00:22:11,440 --> 00:22:22,030
And the problem with continuous integration and concurrency, you know this very well, is that ultimately, no matter how many developers you have working in parallel or seemingly in parallel,

268
00:22:22,030 --> 00:22:24,470
ultimately they all have to go through the same garden gate,

269
00:22:24,470 --> 00:22:26,590
which is it all has to get onto the release branch.

270
00:22:26,600 --> 00:22:32,110
Yeah. And there are two ways you can go about this.

271
00:22:32,110 --> 00:22:35,550
One is the discipline way, which is to have traffic lights and you wait your turn.

272
00:22:35,550 --> 00:22:45,670
So, if you have a build in progress, you don't push on top of that because you might be pushing on top of a broken build or even if it's not a broken build, you might be pushing changes that will conflict.

273
00:22:45,670 --> 00:22:48,910
So, you have to wait and you certainly don't pull changes.

274
00:22:48,910 --> 00:22:51,910
You can't synchronize either way until the build succeeded.

275
00:22:51,910 --> 00:22:57,190
So, if the build takes a few minutes, you're going to end up with every one of those agents lining up in single file.

276
00:22:57,200 --> 00:23:03,510
Yeah. Which I think is where I ended up when I experimented with Claude code with concurrent agents.

277
00:23:03,510 --> 00:23:06,990
I ended up quite quickly and only took me a week to go, "Okay, that doesn't work."

278
00:23:06,990 --> 00:23:11,590
to go back to, "Okay, I'll just have a single process."

279
00:23:11,590 --> 00:23:16,230
And I ended up going back to Ralph loops and then from Ralph loops back to, "Okay, I'll control the process myself."

280
00:23:16,240 --> 00:23:21,030
case, basically, even if the agents were working, let's say, Yeah.

281
00:23:21,040 --> 00:23:23,710
the continuous integration would act as the bottleneck.

282
00:23:23,720 --> 00:23:29,270
Yeah, it's like it doesn't matter how many lanes you add to the motorway that goes to the ferry port in Kent.

283
00:23:29,280 --> 00:23:33,230
There's only one boat and they're all going to go on the boat one at a time.

284
00:23:33,240 --> 00:23:35,230
So, it doesn't matter how many lanes you add.

285
00:23:35,240 --> 00:23:46,630
And so, basically, I guess then from what you're saying that even if we try using some technical practices like you mentioned before with the TDD, continuous testing,

286
00:23:46,630 --> 00:23:48,792
still is a problem because having, say, hundreds of agents,

287
00:23:48,800 --> 00:23:52,230
they will still have the problem of the bottleneck at the CI level.

288
00:23:52,240 --> 00:24:02,870
Yes, and also the fact that they are individually and parallel changing the code with their picture of that code, which is diverging from everybody else's picture.

289
00:24:02,870 --> 00:24:11,510
So, you have that, as with all things concurrent, the real question is to what extent do they depend on each other and therefore how often do they need to synchronize?

290
00:24:11,510 --> 00:24:13,310
And that's Again, it's nothing new.

291
00:24:13,310 --> 00:24:21,030
We still kill the old way, which is, "Okay, then small teams and maybe it's better if the team actually works in a single thread so that we all work together."

292
00:24:21,030 --> 00:24:24,950
This is, I guess, the value proposition of mob programming or teaming,

293
00:24:24,950 --> 00:24:35,030
which is maybe it's better if we bring different expertise to bear at the same time on a single thread of execution rather than trying to have it work in parallel.

294
00:24:35,030 --> 00:24:41,390
And I think we're discovering the same limitation with agentic programming, which is the synchronization problem.

295
00:24:41,400 --> 00:24:44,710
The mythical man-month always gets us in the end.

296
00:24:44,720 --> 00:24:52,910
Do you think that actually having agents working on separate modules or something, if the modularization is done properly, would actually help?

297
00:24:52,920 --> 00:24:58,670
Yes, and this is something that I've tested and seen and I think a lot of other people have as well.

298
00:24:58,670 --> 00:25:04,790
Separation of concerns, modularity, it's always been the secret sauce.

299
00:25:04,790 --> 00:25:12,150
But, when you speed the whole thing up, it becomes so important, especially when it comes to context management,

300
00:25:12,150 --> 00:25:18,990
because the blast radius of the changes you're making has a massive impact on how big the context is going to be.

301
00:25:18,990 --> 00:25:24,750
If you can change one source file and that change is contained to that source file, then you're going to get a small context.

302
00:25:24,750 --> 00:25:33,150
If you don't have that kind of separation of concerns, if changing that file there is going to involve changing all these other files, you've got a big context.

303
00:25:33,160 --> 00:25:36,750
And then you're going to run into all these problems with big contexts.

304
00:25:36,750 --> 00:25:39,990
So, yeah, separation of concerns, super duper important.

305
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:44,430
Several people are saying that AI is an amplifier.

306
00:25:44,430 --> 00:25:51,830
If you are good, we'll help you be maybe a bit better or a lot better, but if you are not so good, you'll actually experience even more trouble.

307
00:25:51,840 --> 00:25:53,790
So, what do you think about that?

308
00:25:53,790 --> 00:25:57,670
And also, how much more productive these teams actually become?

309
00:25:57,680 --> 00:26:01,750
Oh, it's a very good question. So, I can only go on the data here.

310
00:26:01,750 --> 00:26:09,750
So, the Dora folks have found fairly consistently over the last 3 years that, as you say, this technology is an amplifier.

311
00:26:09,750 --> 00:26:17,990
That there is a small percentage of teams that were already high performing who appear to experience a sort of a productivity boost.

312
00:26:17,990 --> 00:26:25,350
Lead times get shorter, throughput gets higher, and and it's not at the expense of the stability of their releases.

313
00:26:25,350 --> 00:26:35,670
What we can't rule out is whether this is a result of the technology or result of what the technology is inflicting on their processes.

314
00:26:35,670 --> 00:26:41,830
That it's a kind of a load test for software development methodology, for the way you do software development.

315
00:26:41,830 --> 00:26:51,670
That if they were Here's the thing, if there are bottlenecks in your process, attaching this code-generating fire hose to your plumbing is going to show up where those bottlenecks are.

316
00:26:51,670 --> 00:26:59,550
And I can't rule out and I've watched teams do this where they said, well, we are taking smaller steps now and we're doing code review more often and so on and so forth.

317
00:26:59,550 --> 00:27:09,510
And I I feel like maybe this technology has highlighted where they were experiencing those kind of bottlenecks to some small extent

318
00:27:09,510 --> 00:27:12,710
and that they've addressed them because they're measuring the outcomes.

319
00:27:12,720 --> 00:27:19,110
And I think that's the important thing. They are measuring things like lead times and they're saying the dials go in the wrong direction.

320
00:27:19,110 --> 00:27:21,110
They go, right, okay, we need to re do code more often.

321
00:27:21,120 --> 00:27:34,631
One thing that's delighted me over the last couple of years is how many teams now who just ignored me on things like mutation testing have suddenly become really interested in mutation testing and really interested in static analysis and speeding up these feedback loops.

322
00:27:34,640 --> 00:27:36,470
So, I can't rule that out.

323
00:27:36,470 --> 00:27:46,350
And if that's the effect that it's having, if the practical effect is that it it's a stress test for your process, as one person put it to me recently, then I don't think that's a bad thing.

324
00:27:46,350 --> 00:27:48,430
It's highlighting areas where you're weak.

325
00:27:48,440 --> 00:27:51,830
Where it is a bad thing is when they're not measuring those outcomes.

326
00:27:51,830 --> 00:27:58,470
So, they're not measuring, to use that analogy of the fire hose, they're connecting the fire hose to their home's plumbing.

327
00:27:58,470 --> 00:28:08,390
They're not measuring the pressure of the water coming out of the shower, they're measuring the pressure of the water coming out of the hose and going, oh, there's lots more water coming out of the hose now, so that's greater productivity.

328
00:28:08,390 --> 00:28:15,790
So, I see all these teams who are measuring more lines of code, more commits, more pull requests, more features, more throughput,

329
00:28:15,790 --> 00:28:22,231
more stuff going into the system and they're not measuring what's coming out of the system and the value of it, what it's worth.

330
00:28:22,231 --> 00:28:28,750
And I think those teams are probably blissfully unaware that this technology is making them worse.

331
00:28:28,750 --> 00:28:30,270
It's an interesting irony, if you think about it.

332
00:28:30,270 --> 00:28:37,070
One of the reasons that they're probably not high performing in that sort of Dora classification is because they don't measure those things.

333
00:28:37,070 --> 00:28:39,190
They don't pay attention to those things.

334
00:28:39,190 --> 00:28:44,990
And because they don't pay attention to those things, they're blissfully unaware that AI is making them worse.

335
00:28:45,000 --> 00:28:48,470
Now, because they're measuring what they can measure easily at least.

336
00:28:48,480 --> 00:28:51,630
Yeah, I see some wonderful posts.

337
00:28:51,630 --> 00:28:57,070
I saw one recently where someone said, "Thanks to Claude Code, we've gone from This is a team of developers.

338
00:28:57,070 --> 00:29:01,350
The team has gone from, I think it was 50 commits a week to 500."

339
00:29:01,360 --> 00:29:03,430
It still doesn't tell you anything about that.

340
00:29:03,440 --> 00:29:08,190
It tells you nothing. It just tells It could be that the batch sizes are much smaller.

341
00:29:08,190 --> 00:29:13,870
I suspect that's not what they're They're actually doing, but you could just say, "Well, we're just committing a few small changes at a time."

342
00:29:13,880 --> 00:29:23,070
And now, a question about working in teams, because when you look around, a lot of these AI advice is seems to be at least aimed at individuals.

343
00:29:23,070 --> 00:29:28,870
Code is produced by teams and has to be done by teams because you can imagine in a company,

344
00:29:28,870 --> 00:29:31,950
if a company hires a single person that say manages 100 agents,

345
00:29:31,960 --> 00:29:33,630
great.

346
00:29:33,630 --> 00:29:35,350
And then this person goes on holiday, what happens?

347
00:29:35,350 --> 00:29:36,950
Or there is a problem, what happens?

348
00:29:36,950 --> 00:29:39,070
So, there has to be some redundancy there.

349
00:29:39,070 --> 00:29:42,070
So, as always to be a team.

350
00:29:42,070 --> 00:29:45,550
Now, how can we integrate these AI tools in a team environment?

351
00:29:45,560 --> 00:29:47,190
Well, what is your take on that?

352
00:29:47,200 --> 00:29:49,070
I've been using them alone.

353
00:29:49,070 --> 00:29:55,510
This is something that I've noted many times that We saw this over this Christmas and New Year.

354
00:29:55,510 --> 00:30:04,830
A lot of chief technology officers and heads of engineering went away over over the Christmas break, and that gave them a bit of time to Well, I better take a look at this Claude Code thing that everyone's talking about.

355
00:30:04,830 --> 00:30:12,070
And they get the proverbial car up to 200 miles an hour on a straight road with no other traffic around.

356
00:30:12,070 --> 00:30:13,870
And they come back I saw this.

357
00:30:13,870 --> 00:30:18,991
They came back into the office in January, and they went to their teams and they said, "You will use this. This is the way it's going to be.

358
00:30:19,000 --> 00:30:21,390
I was so productive."

359
00:30:21,390 --> 00:30:30,671
Working by yourself with no customer, um no team, no other stakeholders, no regulations, no legacy code, no dependencies,

360
00:30:30,671 --> 00:30:33,030
well done you.

361
00:30:33,030 --> 00:30:34,710
Yes, you can get the car up to 200 miles per hour.

362
00:30:34,710 --> 00:30:39,470
That's no one's denying that these things can generate code really fast.

363
00:30:39,470 --> 00:30:43,790
But most software development teams are driving through heavy traffic.

364
00:30:43,790 --> 00:30:52,750
And living in London, I know for a fact that doesn't matter how fast your car is, you're going to be going at best 9 mph, sort of average speed.

365
00:30:52,750 --> 00:30:54,990
A bicycle is faster than a Bugatti.

366
00:30:54,990 --> 00:30:57,590
Faster cars does not equal faster traffic.

367
00:30:57,590 --> 00:31:06,950
I think that the mistake that so many people have made is that they've taken what is a local optimization, code generation, writing code,

368
00:31:06,950 --> 00:31:13,110
and it's kind of our fault as an industry as well because we've so often equated software development with coding,

369
00:31:13,110 --> 00:31:15,150
whereas in reality coding is just a small part.

370
00:31:15,150 --> 00:31:19,350
Coding is the part where you are driving forwards in the rush hour traffic.

371
00:31:19,350 --> 00:31:26,110
But most of your journey is waiting, waiting for input, waiting for feedback, etc. etc. And we've made a terrible mistake,

372
00:31:26,110 --> 00:31:30,070
but also remembering that there are other cars on the road.

373
00:31:30,070 --> 00:31:34,470
That a lot of what we do when we're driving through cities or towns is try not to bump into each other.

374
00:31:34,470 --> 00:31:43,190
That we're dealing with dependencies and mergers and conflicts and and competing concerns, and that's what takes up all the time.

375
00:31:43,190 --> 00:31:45,230
And coding was only ever such a small part of it.

376
00:31:45,230 --> 00:31:50,630
I remember when the software crafts from ship movement sort of really took off in 2006-2007,

377
00:31:50,640 --> 00:31:55,350
there was this sudden fascination with everyone typing faster.

378
00:31:55,360 --> 00:31:59,510
They're all on the Mavis Beacon typing tutor course.

379
00:31:59,510 --> 00:32:01,550
And we need to write code faster.

380
00:32:01,550 --> 00:32:08,150
And I remember saying, apart from it being ableist, cuz I know some fantastic software developers who really get stuff done,

381
00:32:08,150 --> 00:32:10,350
but because of certain circumstances can only type for example,

382
00:32:10,360 --> 00:32:12,430
with two fingers at a time.

383
00:32:12,430 --> 00:32:17,350
But at the end of a session, they've still got just as much if not more done in real terms.

384
00:32:17,360 --> 00:32:22,670
So I had a problem with it for that, but the main problem I had was typing code was never the bottleneck.

385
00:32:22,670 --> 00:32:27,190
It's never been the bottleneck, not since we were punching holes in cards.

386
00:32:27,190 --> 00:32:29,630
It's never been a real problem.

387
00:32:29,630 --> 00:32:31,710
Looking things up has never been the bottleneck.

388
00:32:31,710 --> 00:32:35,790
Finding out how to call this API or use that library has never really been the bottleneck.

389
00:32:35,800 --> 00:32:43,990
The bottleneck has always been the part that does the heavy lifting, the dependencies, that the communication, the feedback loops,

390
00:32:43,990 --> 00:32:49,271
and the big one, the really big lever that that no team wants to pull, which is batch size.

391
00:32:49,271 --> 00:32:53,030
Try as I might to persuade them, say, "Just take a smaller step."

392
00:32:53,030 --> 00:32:54,750
They go, "But that's It feels so slow, Jason.

393
00:32:54,750 --> 00:32:56,910
It's slowing me down."

394
00:32:56,910 --> 00:32:58,991
And yet, weirdly, we've already delivered. It's already shipped.

395
00:32:59,000 --> 00:33:07,710
And now, we said that basically the teams that are getting the most out of these tools are the teams that are already working in a certain way, yeah.

396
00:33:07,710 --> 00:33:14,910
