but it's got to go in the eyes, through the brain, and out the fingers, I think,

594
00:49:07,752 --> 00:49:12,030
to engage those centers of your brain that will actually that makes it sink in.

595
00:49:12,040 --> 00:49:17,470
It's like it's like everyone's on speed dial and no one can remember anybody's number.

596
00:49:17,470 --> 00:49:21,470
And the moment you take that that phone away, they can't remember anybody's number.

597
00:49:21,470 --> 00:49:30,750
And I'm seeing this more and more now, and it is from personal experience it does really appear to be accelerating, but there's also research that is suggesting that it really is accelerating.

598
00:49:30,750 --> 00:49:34,790
And I I really worry about that because that's going to hit another problem,

599
00:49:34,790 --> 00:49:38,910
which is that regardless of their ability to comprehend any code,

600
00:49:38,910 --> 00:49:43,390
is the fact that more and more code is being produced that nobody's even looked at.

601
00:49:43,390 --> 00:49:51,630
So, they understand code slower, and there's going to be lots more code that needs to be understood when it inevitably breaks and the language model can't fix it.

602
00:49:51,640 --> 00:49:57,230
Oh, no, another question may be related to this, you know, to the cognitive ability and the skills.

603
00:49:57,230 --> 00:50:05,350
Now, the most recent development for using AI is the spec-driven development trend, where people are supposed to write specifications in natural language,

604
00:50:05,350 --> 00:50:10,430
and the the system will will create something based on that.

605
00:50:10,430 --> 00:50:15,950
Now, if this works out, maybe we don't need to know coding that much after all. What do you think?

606
00:50:15,960 --> 00:50:23,590
So, there are different interpretations of what people mean by spec-driven Well, and I think I think probably the mainstream one that I see a lot.

607
00:50:23,590 --> 00:50:27,950
I say mainstream, it's mainstream on LinkedIn, which means it's probably not mainstream.

608
00:50:27,950 --> 00:50:37,750
But, the one I see most often on LinkedIn is along those lines, that you write natural language specifications, and then you feed it to Claude code or Cursors or whatever or Lovable,

609
00:50:37,750 --> 00:50:39,910
and off it goes and chugs away at it and produces you a working system.

610
00:50:39,920 --> 00:50:47,750
It kind of sort of works, provided you you're not particularly attached to any particular end result.

611
00:50:47,750 --> 00:50:52,310
It will produce This is the problem with natural languages, they are inherently ambiguous.

612
00:50:52,310 --> 00:50:54,030
And that's an unfixable problem.

613
00:50:54,030 --> 00:50:56,150
So, it will produce an interpretation.

614
00:50:56,150 --> 00:51:00,510
As long as you're happy with the interpretation it produces, then that's okay.

615
00:51:00,510 --> 00:51:10,070
Under the hood, the code it's likely to generate is going to be problematic in terms of So, large language models are really good at generating code, but they're really bad at modifying later.

616
00:51:10,080 --> 00:51:21,310
That is actually an interesting technical Actually, it seems that also again, some research and on that it seems that the better the quality of the code,

617
00:51:21,310 --> 00:51:24,150
the better the agents are able to help with modifying and refactoring that later.

618
00:51:24,160 --> 00:51:34,030
I see people saying, for example, they claim, "Oh, code doesn't need to be readable by humans anymore because of AI." But, then you get I'm looking at my bookmarks here.

619
00:51:34,030 --> 00:51:40,110
I've got another great study here called "How does naming affect language models on code analysis tasks?"

620
00:51:40,110 --> 00:51:42,150
So, this is a study that's relatively recent.

621
00:51:42,150 --> 00:51:43,670
And I've done this experiment.

622
00:51:43,670 --> 00:51:46,910
It was done It was This study is only a couple of years old, maybe a year and a half old.

623
00:51:46,910 --> 00:51:51,870
And I've done this experiment myself where you take you take code that is pretty easy to understand or is intuitive to me.

624
00:51:51,880 --> 00:51:56,430
It's you know, fairly easy to comprehend.

625
00:51:56,430 --> 00:52:05,150
And then you take it through a series of iterations where you obfuscate the code more and more and measure just how, you know, obfuscated it is.

626
00:52:05,150 --> 00:52:10,510
So you're changing function names and class names and everything until they make no sense whatsoever.

627
00:52:10,510 --> 00:52:13,950
And then you give the model tasks um to conduct.

628
00:52:13,950 --> 00:52:17,390
And then you measure measure completion or how many of those tasks are complete.

629
00:52:17,390 --> 00:52:19,190
And it does indeed go off a cliff.

630
00:52:19,190 --> 00:52:24,430
The less intuitive the code is to a human being, the more I think the clue really is language model.

631
00:52:24,440 --> 00:52:28,030
That should be our first clue. They are language models.

632
00:52:28,030 --> 00:52:33,670
They were originally created invented to translate human languages.

633
00:52:33,670 --> 00:52:42,310
And when you start using language in the code that doesn't match the language of the problem, the language you're trying to quote, all kinds of things go wrong.

634
00:52:42,310 --> 00:52:44,430
So obviously that affects model performance.

635
00:52:44,440 --> 00:52:46,270
Complexity has a massive impact.

636
00:52:46,280 --> 00:52:55,110
Coupling and cohesion in modular design has a huge impact as we we discussed in terms of the potential blast radius of changes,

637
00:52:55,110 --> 00:52:58,430
how much code needs to be brought into the conduct context that has to be need to be changed.

638
00:52:58,440 --> 00:53:00,750
Duplication has a similar effect.

639
00:53:00,750 --> 00:53:08,590
So all the things that we've been saying for years make code harder to change or harder to understand and harder to change for humans,

640
00:53:08,590 --> 00:53:11,390
including fast running regression tests,

641
00:53:11,390 --> 00:53:20,470
are exactly the same factors that research and experiment experiences telling us make code harder to change for large language models.

